WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.672
Categorical values often contain several labels for what may be the same concept.

00:00:06.672 --> 00:00:12.207
For example, a product dataset might contain electronics, electronic, and tech.

00:00:12.207 --> 00:00:14.847
We may want all three to appear as Electronics.

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:21.941
But SQL cannot know that these labels mean the same thing unless we define that rule.

00:00:22.456 --> 00:00:27.008
This is why category standardization is partly a business decision.

00:00:27.008 --> 00:00:34.273
The database can apply the mapping, but a person or organization must define what the mapping means.

00:00:34.273 --> 00:00:38.133
Imagine another category called accessories.

00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:40.723
Should it be grouped with electronics?

00:00:40.723 --> 00:00:44.758
Maybe yes in one company and no in another.

00:00:44.758 --> 00:00:53.933
So before creating mappings, review the observed values and document which source labels belong to each target category.

00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:58.573
Once that rule is clear, SQL can enforce it consistently.
